ML_PAGE("t1-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l1\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 1 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Topic 1 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 30 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>With worked reasoning</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l1\" data-quiz=\"1\">Start Topic 1 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l1\" id=\"t1p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">26 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - CONCEPTS &amp; DISTINCTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Part 1 - The MCQ mental model\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s2\" title=\"Part 1 - The MCQ mental model\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P1</span>The MCQ mental model</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 - High-yield distinctions you must be able to identify instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s3\" title=\"Part 2 - High-yield distinctions you must be able to identify instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P2</span>High-yield distinctions</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Traditional risk definition vs broader risk definition\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s4\" title=\"3. Traditional risk definition vs broader risk definition\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Traditional vs broader definition</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Loss exposure vs actual loss\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s5\" title=\"4. Loss exposure vs actual loss\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Loss exposure vs actual loss</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Frequency vs severity\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s6\" title=\"5. Frequency vs severity\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Frequency vs severity</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Avoidance vs loss prevention vs loss reduction\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s7\" title=\"6. Avoidance vs loss prevention vs loss reduction\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Avoidance vs loss prevention vs loss reduction</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Pure vs speculative risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s8\" title=\"7. Pure vs speculative risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Pure vs speculative risk</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s9\" title=\"8. Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Diversification rule\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s10\" title=\"9. Diversification rule\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Diversification rule</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Beta\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s11\" title=\"10. Beta\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Beta</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Enterprise risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s12\" title=\"11. Enterprise risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Enterprise risk</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Operational risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s13\" title=\"12. Operational risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Operational risk</button></li><li data-full=\"13. Liquidity risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s14\" title=\"13. Liquidity risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Liquidity risk</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Credit risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s15\" title=\"14. Credit risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Credit risk</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Market risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s16\" title=\"15. Market risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Market risk</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Business risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s17\" title=\"16. Business risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Business risk</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Reputation risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s18\" title=\"17. Reputation risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Reputation risk</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Resource risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s19\" title=\"18. Resource risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Resource risk</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - TRAPS</li><li data-full=\"Part 3 - The major MCQ traps\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s21\" title=\"Part 3 - The major MCQ traps\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P3</span>The major MCQ traps</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - MOCK MCQs &amp; ANSWERS</li><li data-full=\"Part 4 - Mock MCQ Set 1\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s23\" title=\"Part 4 - Mock MCQ Set 1\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P4</span>Mock MCQ Set 1</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 - Application questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s24\" title=\"Part 5 - Application questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P5</span>Application questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 6 - Analysis-level MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s25\" title=\"Part 6 - Analysis-level MCQs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P6</span>Analysis-level MCQs</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 7 - Answers and reasoning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s26\" title=\"Part 7 - Answers and reasoning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P7</span>Answers and reasoning</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D - HARDER QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Part 8 - Harder &quot;two answers look correct&quot; questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s28\" title=\"Part 8 - Harder &quot;two answers look correct&quot; questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P8</span>Harder \"two answers\" questions</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART E - FINAL REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Part 9 - What I would memorise the night before\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s30\" title=\"Part 9 - What I would memorise the night before\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P9</span>What I would memorise the night before</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 10 - The strategy I want you to use in the actual MCQ\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t1p-s31\" title=\"Part 10 - The strategy I want you to use in the actual MCQ\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P10</span>Strategy in the actual MCQ</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l1\" open><summary><div><strong>Topic 1 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Distinctions, traps and practice questions, section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>26 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>For <strong>Topic 1</strong>, I would prepare you for MCQ very differently from essay revision.</p>\n<p>MCQs are dangerous because the lecturer can give you <strong>four statements that all sound vaguely correct</strong>, but only one matches the exact dimension being tested. So instead of memorising definitions, you need to recognise the <strong>decision rule behind each concept</strong>.</p>\n<p>Topic 1 itself centres on the definition of risk and uncertainty, the impact of risk on organisations, and types/dimensions of risk.</p>\n<p>I would prepare using this hierarchy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Concept → Distinction → Trap → Scenario → Decision\"><span>Concept</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Distinction</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Trap</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Scenario</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Decision</span></div><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t1p-d1\">PART A - CONCEPTS &amp; DISTINCTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 - The MCQ mental model</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_concept_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The chain runs left to right; the five classification questions each describe one link.</figcaption></figure></div><p>When you see an unfamiliar scenario, do <strong>not</strong> immediately look for a keyword.</p>\n<p>Ask these questions in order:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{1.\\ What is the objective?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{2.\\ What uncertain event/exposure exists?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{3.\\ Is this loss-only or gain/loss?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{4.\\ Is it firm-specific or economy-wide?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{5.\\ Is the question about probability or severity?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{6.\\ Is management avoiding, preventing or reducing?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{7.\\ What is the root risk rather than merely the consequence?}$$</div>\n<p>That process will solve a surprisingly large percentage of Topic 1 questions.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 - High-yield distinctions you must be able to identify instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>1. Risk vs uncertainty</h3>\n<p>Your slides distinguish them as:</p>\n<p><strong>Risk:</strong> possible outcomes are known and the odds may be known.</p>\n<p><strong>Uncertainty:</strong> possible outcomes themselves may not be known, let alone their probabilities.</p>\n<h4>MCQ shortcut</h4>\n<p>If the question gives:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We know X, Y and Z can happen and can estimate probabilities.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk</span></div>\n<p>If it says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We don't even know all the possible outcomes.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Uncertainty</span></div>\n<h4>Trap</h4>\n<p>Do not choose uncertainty simply because:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The future isn't certain.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That would make practically everything uncertainty and render the distinction useless.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Traditional risk definition vs broader risk definition</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_three_definitions\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The traditional and finance definitions are special cases; ERM uses the broader ISO definition.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Traditional framing in the slides:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk} = \\text{chance of loss/harm}$$</div>\n<p>The broader definition:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk = effect of uncertainty on objectives</span></div>\n<p>and that effect may be positive, negative or a deviation from expectations.</p>\n<h4>MCQ trap</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Risk always produces a negative outcome.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>False.</strong></p>\n<p>Under the broader definition, risk can create positive or negative deviation.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Loss exposure vs actual loss</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Loss exposure means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a situation or circumstance where loss is possible, whether or not loss actually occurs.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Loss exposure} \\neq \\text{loss}}$$</div>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>A factory is located in an earthquake zone.</p>\n<p>No earthquake happens.</p>\n<p>The factory still had:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>loss exposure</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Frequency vs severity</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_risk_matrix\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Frequency x severity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Set how often and how badly a loss could strike; the matrix suggests where management attention and which control belong.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Your slides define:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Frequency} = \\text{probable number of losses}}$$</div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Severity} = \\text{probable size of loss}}$$</div>\n<h4>Fast test</h4>\n<p>\"How often?\"</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{Frequency}}$$</div>\n<p>\"How bad?\"</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{Severity}}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Avoidance vs loss prevention vs loss reduction</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_treatment_ladder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Avoidance removes the exposure (when losses would be frequent and severe and the activity is not essential); prevention cuts frequency; reduction cuts severity.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is almost guaranteed MCQ material.</p>\n<h3>Avoidance</h3>\n<p>Do not undertake the exposure.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(\\text{loss})=0$$</div>\n<p>for that activity.</p>\n<h3>Loss prevention</h3>\n<p>Reduce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Frequency</span></div>\n<h3>Loss reduction</h3>\n<p>Reduce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Severity</span></div>\n<p>The slides explicitly distinguish prevention as reducing frequency and reduction as reducing severity.</p>\n<h4>Memorise this:</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Avoidance} &amp;\\rightarrow \\text{remove exposure}\\\\\n\\text{Prevention} &amp;\\rightarrow \\text{less likely}\\\\\n\\text{Reduction} &amp;\\rightarrow \\text{less damaging}\n\\end{aligned}}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Pure vs speculative risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_payoff\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Pure risk has no upside; speculative risk spreads across gain, no change and loss.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This classification asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What outcomes are possible?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Pure risk</h3>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss or no loss</span></div>\n<p>No upside.</p>\n<p>Examples include accidents and natural hazards.</p>\n<h3>Speculative risk</h3>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Gain or loss</span></div>\n<p>Examples include stock-market and real-estate investments.</p>\n<h4>Trap</h4>\n<p>\"High risk\" does <strong>not</strong> mean speculative.</p>\n<p>\"Accidental\" does <strong>not</strong> automatically mean pure because of severity.</p>\n<p>Look at the possible outcomes.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Diversifiable vs non-diversifiable</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This classification asks a completely different question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Can diversification remove it?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Diversifiable risk</h3>\n<p>Also called:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>non-systematic,</li>\n<li>particular,</li>\n<li>firm-specific.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It affects individuals or relatively small groups rather than the whole market.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Company-specific</span></div>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>lawsuit,</p>\n<p>labour strike,</p>\n<p>CEO failure,</p>\n<p>company-specific innovation.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Non-diversifiable risk</h3>\n<p>Also called:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>systematic / market / fundamental risk</span></div>\n<p>It affects the wider economy or broad groups of assets.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<p>interest rates,</p>\n<p>recession,</p>\n<p>inflation shock,</p>\n<p>economic cycle.</p>\n<p>The slides give examples such as business cycles, inflation shocks and interest-rate changes.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Diversification rule</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_diversification\"><div class=\"vis-title\">How diversification changes total portfolio risk</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Add assets and change how alike they are; firm-specific risk fades, the systematic floor stays.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Diversification reduces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>unsystematic risk</span></div>\n<p>but not:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>systematic risk</span></div>\n<p>The slides explicitly state that company-specific surprises can offset one another across a large portfolio, whereas economy-wide risks tend to move assets in the same direction.</p>\n<p>This is one of the highest-probability exam concepts.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Beta</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides state beta measures an asset's relative systematic risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta&gt;1$$</div>\n<p>means more systematic risk than average.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta&lt;1$$</div>\n<p>means less systematic risk than average.</p>\n<h4>MCQ trap</h4>\n<p>Beta does <strong>not</strong> primarily measure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>credit risk,</li>\n<li>firm-specific risk,</li>\n<li>liquidity risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It relates to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>systematic risk</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Enterprise risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_category_finder\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Ask where the risk originates; the answer names the enterprise category.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Enterprise risk encompasses major risks faced by the organisation, including pure, speculative and financial risks, and can affect operations, activities and reporting.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>whole-company perspective</span></div>\n<p>rather than one narrow category.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Operational risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides associate operational risk with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>internal-control failure,</li>\n<li>technological failure,</li>\n<li>mismanagement,</li>\n<li>human error,</li>\n<li>inadequate training.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They also place:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Fraud risk</span></div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Model risk</span></div>\n<p>under operational risk.</p>\n<h4>Shortcut</h4>\n<p>If something goes wrong in:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>people + process + systems + controls</span></div>\n<p>think operational risk.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Liquidity risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t1_liquidity\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>A firm can be rich in assets and still fail to pay tomorrow&#x27;s obligation.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Core question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Can the firm get the cash needed when it needs it?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The slides distinguish:</p>\n<h4>Asset liquidity risk</h4>\n<p>Difficulty buying/selling an asset.</p>\n<h4>Funding liquidity risk</h4>\n<p>Unable to meet short-term obligations when due.</p>\n<h4>Important trap</h4>\n<p>A company can be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>wealthy but illiquid</span></div>\n<p>because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Assets} \\neq \\text{cash available now}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Credit risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Credit risk concerns the possibility that a creditor receives payment late or not at all.</p>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Who owes whom money?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If the problem is that the counterparty may fail to repay:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Credit risk</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Market risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t1_market_moves\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Directional versus volatility risk</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · The same exchange-rate path seen as directional risk (where it ends) or volatility risk (how much it swings).</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slides connect market risk to movements in prices of financial instruments and distinguish:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Directional risk</span></div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Non-directional / volatility risk</span></div>\n<p>Directional:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is the price/rate going up or down?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Non-directional:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How volatile is it?</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Business risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Business risk concerns inadequate profits due to uncertainty and is influenced by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>consumer preferences,</li>\n<li>demand,</li>\n<li>sales volume,</li>\n<li>input costs,</li>\n<li>competition,</li>\n<li>economic conditions,</li>\n<li>regulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Can the business model generate sufficient profit?</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Reputation risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Reputational risk concerns damage to the organisation's standing when stakeholder expectations are not met. The slides distinguish:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>direct,</li>\n<li>indirect,</li>\n<li>tangential</li>\n</ul>\n<p>sources.</p>\n<h4>Useful distinction</h4>\n<p>Employee causes scandal:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Indirect</span></div>\n<p>Company itself acts badly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Direct</span></div>\n<p>Supplier/JV partner damages reputation:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Tangential</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Resource risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Resource risk occurs when objectives cannot be achieved because resources are insufficient or unavailable.</p>\n<p>Resources include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>financing,</li>\n<li>time,</li>\n<li>skilled labour,</li>\n<li>other required inputs.</li>\n</ul></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t1p-d20\">PART B - TRAPS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 - The major MCQ traps</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These are the distinctions I would expect a lecturer to exploit.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>If the question asks...</th>\n<th>Think...</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gain vs loss/no-loss</td>\n<td>Pure vs speculative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Firm-specific vs economy-wide</td>\n<td>Diversifiable vs systematic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How often?</td>\n<td>Frequency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How bad?</td>\n<td>Severity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Make event less likely</td>\n<td>Prevention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Make consequences smaller</td>\n<td>Reduction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Do not undertake activity</td>\n<td>Avoidance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cannot repay amount owed</td>\n<td>Credit risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cannot meet cash obligations now</td>\n<td>Funding liquidity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cannot sell asset easily</td>\n<td>Asset liquidity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System/process/person failure</td>\n<td>Operational risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business cannot earn enough profit</td>\n<td>Business risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reputation harmed by supplier</td>\n<td>Tangential reputation risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Economy-wide interest-rate change</td>\n<td>Systematic risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CEO unexpectedly resigns</td>\n<td>Unsystematic risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t1p-d22\">PART C - MOCK MCQs &amp; ANSWERS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 - Mock MCQ Set 1</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Try these <strong>before looking at the answers</strong>.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>A company's objective is to achieve RM50 million profit. Unexpectedly strong demand increases profit to RM65 million.</p>\n<p>Under the broader definition of risk, this situation represents:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> No risk because profit increased</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risk because uncertainty caused deviation from the objective</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Pure risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Loss exposure only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Risk is the effect of uncertainty on objectives and can involve positive deviation.</p>\n<p>The trap is thinking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>positive outcome = no risk.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>A company knows that a machine may fail, operate normally, or operate at reduced capacity, and it can estimate probabilities for each outcome.</p>\n<p>This situation is best classified as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Uncertainty</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Speculative uncertainty</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Systemic risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Possible outcomes and probabilities are estimable.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>A newly invented technology could fundamentally reshape an industry, but companies do not yet know all the possible outcomes or their probabilities.</p>\n<p>This most closely represents:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pure risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Market risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Uncertainty</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Loss reduction</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Possible outcomes themselves are unclear.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Uncertainty</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>A warehouse is located in an earthquake zone, although no earthquake has occurred.</p>\n<p>The warehouse represents:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Actual loss</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Loss exposure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Loss reduction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Speculative risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Loss is possible even though none occurred.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss exposure</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A retailer experiences approximately 200 minor theft incidents every year.</p>\n<p>The \"200 incidents\" primarily measures:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Severity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Frequency</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Loss reduction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Systematic risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Number of incidents:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Frequency</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>A manufacturer installs automatic sprinklers so that if fire occurs, damage is contained.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Avoidance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Loss prevention</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Loss reduction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Risk retention</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Sprinklers operate after fire begins to reduce damage.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Severity} \\downarrow}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss reduction</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>A manufacturer introduces safety training intended to decrease the number of workplace accidents.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Loss reduction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Loss prevention</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Avoidance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Speculation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Training aims to prevent accidents.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Frequency} \\downarrow}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss prevention</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>A company decides not to enter a politically unstable country at all.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Loss prevention</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Loss reduction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Avoidance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Diversification</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The exposure is never undertaken.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Avoidance</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Which is the clearest example of pure risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buying shares</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Purchasing real estate hoping its price rises</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A factory being damaged by fire</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Entering a new foreign market</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Fire gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{loss or no loss}$$</div>\n<p>No upside.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>Which is the clearest speculative risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Employee injury</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Earthquake</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Investing in shares</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Theft</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Shares can produce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{gain or loss}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore speculative.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 - Application questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>Company A's CEO resigns unexpectedly, causing its share price to fall. Other firms are largely unaffected.</p>\n<p>This is primarily:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Systematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Non-diversifiable risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Unsystematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Market-wide risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>CEO departure affects one company.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Unsystematic / firm-specific</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>Bank Negara unexpectedly raises interest rates and share prices across many industries decline.</p>\n<p>This is primarily:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Diversifiable risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Firm-specific risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Systematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Management risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Interest rates affect broad parts of the economy.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Systematic</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>An investor owns 100 unrelated companies instead of one company.</p>\n<p>Which risk should be substantially reduced?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Systematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Unsystematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Inflation risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Interest-rate risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Diversification primarily reduces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Unsystematic risk</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>Which risk would generally <strong>not</strong> disappear through diversification?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Employee strike at Company X</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Lawsuit against Company Y</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> National recession</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Failure of Company Z's new product</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A recession affects many companies in the same general direction.</p>\n<p>Therefore it cannot simply be diversified away.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A share has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta = 1.4$$</div>\n<p>According to your chapter, this means the asset has:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> No systematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Less systematic risk than average</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> More systematic risk than average</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> More unsystematic risk than average</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta&gt;1$$</div>\n<p>means above-average systematic risk according to your slides.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>A payment-system outage prevents customers from completing transactions.</p>\n<p>The most immediate risk category is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Credit risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Operational risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Market risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Speculative risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A technology/system failure affecting transactions is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Operational risk</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>A bank's loan-approval model systematically approves borrowers who are unlikely to repay because the model is badly designed.</p>\n<p>The defective model itself represents:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Market risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Resource risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Model/operational risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pure credit risk only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The root cause is a flawed model.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Model risk / operational risk</span></div>\n<p>Credit losses may be the <strong>consequence</strong>.</p>\n<p>This root-cause distinction is exactly the sort of thing MCQs exploit.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>A borrower cannot repay a RM500,000 loan.</p>\n<p>From the lender's perspective this is primarily:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Market risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Credit risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Resource risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Asset liquidity risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The borrower owes the lender money and may not repay.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Credit risk</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>A company owns RM20 million of property but does not have enough cash to pay salaries due tomorrow.</p>\n<p>This primarily illustrates:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Credit risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Funding liquidity risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Asset liquidity risk only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Market risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The company cannot meet immediate obligations.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Funding liquidity risk</span></div>\n<p>Do not confuse asset wealth with cash availability.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>A company wants to sell a specialised asset immediately, but there are almost no buyers.</p>\n<p>This primarily illustrates:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Funding liquidity risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Asset liquidity risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Credit risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Business risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Difficulty selling an asset:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Asset liquidity risk</span></div></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 - Analysis-level MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These are closer to what can separate students who memorised from students who understand.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>A restaurant chain suffers a viral backlash after consumers object to the behaviour of one of its suppliers. Sales subsequently fall.</p>\n<p>What is the most accurate analysis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Only operational risk is present</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Tangential reputational risk contributes to business risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is purely systematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The supplier creates asset liquidity risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The supplier is a peripheral party, so the reputational risk is tangential under the chapter's terminology. The subsequent sales decline creates business consequences.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>A company cuts quality-control spending to minimise costs. Product defects subsequently increase and customers begin leaving.</p>\n<p>Which chain best describes the situation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Credit risk → liquidity risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Resource risk → systematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Cost decision → quality risk → customer/business risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Market risk → speculative risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The cost-cutting decision weakens quality control:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Quality problems → Customer loss → Business impact\"><span>Quality problems</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Customer loss</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Business impact</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A pharmaceutical company spends heavily on R&amp;D, but its new product fails commercially.</p>\n<p>According to the chapter's discussion of diversifiable risk, this would most closely represent:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Systematic economic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Business/firm-specific risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Non-diversifiable inflation risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pure risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>This is specific to the company's business activity rather than an economy-wide event.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>A company has excellent assets but cannot convert them into cash quickly enough to meet immediate obligations.</p>\n<p>Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The company is insolvent by definition</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The company has no financial risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The company can be asset-rich but liquidity-constrained</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The situation must be credit risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>This is exactly why:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Assets} \\neq \\text{liquidity}$$</div>\n<p>A company may have substantial wealth but insufficient immediate cash.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>Which statement best explains why investors are generally not compensated merely for taking high unsystematic risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Unsystematic risk cannot cause losses</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It can largely be reduced through diversification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Beta measures unsystematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Systematic risk disappears in large portfolios</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>If investors can eliminate a risk relatively cheaply through diversification, simply choosing not to diversify does not make that exposure economically special.</p>\n<p>Your slides therefore emphasise systematic rather than unsystematic risk when discussing risk compensation.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 - Answers and reasoning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Don't just check whether you got them right. Check <strong>why the other options are wrong</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Q1 - B</h3>\n<p>Risk is the effect of uncertainty on objectives and can involve positive deviation.</p>\n<p>The trap is thinking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>positive outcome = no risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q2 - B</h3>\n<p>Possible outcomes and probabilities are estimable.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q3 - C</h3>\n<p>Possible outcomes themselves are unclear.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Uncertainty</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q4 - B</h3>\n<p>Loss is possible even though none occurred.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss exposure</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q5 - B</h3>\n<p>Number of incidents:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Frequency</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q6 - C</h3>\n<p>Sprinklers operate after fire begins to reduce damage.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Severity} \\downarrow}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss reduction</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q7 - B</h3>\n<p>Training aims to prevent accidents.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Frequency} \\downarrow}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Loss prevention</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q8 - C</h3>\n<p>The exposure is never undertaken.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Avoidance</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q9 - C</h3>\n<p>Fire gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{loss or no loss}$$</div>\n<p>No upside.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q10 - C</h3>\n<p>Shares can produce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{gain or loss}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore speculative.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q11 - C</h3>\n<p>CEO departure affects one company.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Unsystematic / firm-specific</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q12 - C</h3>\n<p>Interest rates affect broad parts of the economy.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Systematic</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q13 - B</h3>\n<p>Diversification primarily reduces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Unsystematic risk</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q14 - C</h3>\n<p>A recession affects many companies in the same general direction.</p>\n<p>Therefore it cannot simply be diversified away.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q15 - C</h3>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\beta&gt;1$$</div>\n<p>means above-average systematic risk according to your slides.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q16 - B</h3>\n<p>A technology/system failure affecting transactions is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Operational risk</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q17 - C</h3>\n<p>The root cause is a flawed model.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Model risk / operational risk</span></div>\n<p>Credit losses may be the <strong>consequence</strong>.</p>\n<p>This root-cause distinction is exactly the sort of thing MCQs exploit.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q18 - B</h3>\n<p>The borrower owes the lender money and may not repay.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Credit risk</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q19 - B</h3>\n<p>The company cannot meet immediate obligations.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Funding liquidity risk</span></div>\n<p>Do not confuse asset wealth with cash availability.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q20 - B</h3>\n<p>Difficulty selling an asset:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Asset liquidity risk</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q21 - B</h3>\n<p>The supplier is a peripheral party, so the reputational risk is tangential under the chapter's terminology. The subsequent sales decline creates business consequences.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q22 - C</h3>\n<p>The cost-cutting decision weakens quality control:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Quality problems → Customer loss → Business impact\"><span>Quality problems</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Customer loss</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Business impact</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q23 - B</h3>\n<p>This is specific to the company's business activity rather than an economy-wide event.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q24 - C</h3>\n<p>This is exactly why:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Assets} \\neq \\text{liquidity}$$</div>\n<p>A company may have substantial wealth but insufficient immediate cash.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q25 - B</h3>\n<p>If investors can eliminate a risk relatively cheaply through diversification, simply choosing not to diversify does not make that exposure economically special.</p>\n<p>Your slides therefore emphasise systematic rather than unsystematic risk when discussing risk compensation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t1p-d27\">PART D - HARDER QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 - Harder \"two answers look correct\" questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These are the ones I would expect in a technical MCQ.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>A company uses a single supplier. The supplier suddenly shuts down, causing production to stop.</p>\n<p>Which answer identifies the <strong>most immediate underlying exposure</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reputational risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Resource/supplier risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Funding liquidity risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Credit risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Production stoppage may eventually cause:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{financial}$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{reputation}$$</div>\n<p>problems.</p>\n<p>But the original exposure is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>resource/supplier dependency</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>A factory fire causes production to stop. Customers become angry, social-media criticism increases and sales decline.</p>\n<p>Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The event can belong to multiple risk classifications depending on the dimension analysed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Every event must belong to exactly one risk category</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The event is necessarily speculative risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because reputation is affected, the fire is not pure risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>This is an extremely important principle.</p>\n<p>The fire can simultaneously be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Pure risk}$$</div>\n<p>under one classification,</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>operational/business exposure</span></div>\n<p>under another,</p>\n<p>and generate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{reputational consequences}$$</div>\n<p>afterwards.</p>\n<p>Different classifications ask different questions.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>An investor owns 200 technology companies. A government announces an economy-wide interest-rate increase.</p>\n<p>Why might diversification fail to eliminate the resulting risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> There are too many assets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The event affects many assets in the same direction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Technology firms have no unsystematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Diversification increases systematic risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Diversification works best when company-specific events are imperfectly related.</p>\n<p>Systematic events affect many companies simultaneously.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>Which is the best example of <strong>loss prevention rather than loss reduction</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fireproof walls limiting damage after a fire</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sprinklers suppressing a fire</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Driver training reducing accident occurrence</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Emergency funds helping a firm recover after disaster</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The wording:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>reducing accident occurrence</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>means:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Frequency}\\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Therefore prevention.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>A company sells goods on credit. One customer cannot pay. Because several other customers also delay payments, the company itself cannot meet payroll.</p>\n<p>Which sequence is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Market risk → systematic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Credit risk → funding liquidity risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Asset liquidity risk → pure risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Resource risk → market risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>First:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{customers do not repay}\n=\n\\boxed{\\text{Credit risk}}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{company cannot meet short-term cash obligation}\n=\n\\boxed{\\text{Funding liquidity risk}}$$</div>\n<p>This is precisely how one risk can trigger another.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t1p-d29\">PART E - FINAL REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 - What I would memorise the night before</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Not every slide deserves equal brain space. These relationships do.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Risk} = \\text{effect of uncertainty on objectives}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Risk vs Uncertainty}\n=\n\\text{known possible outcomes vs unknown possible outcomes}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Pure} = \\text{loss/no loss}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Speculative} = \\text{gain/loss}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Diversifiable} = \\text{firm-specific} = \\text{unsystematic}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Non-diversifiable} = \\text{market-wide} = \\text{systematic}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Diversification removes unsystematic, not systematic risk</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\beta \\rightarrow \\text{systematic risk}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Frequency} = \\text{how often}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Severity} = \\text{how bad}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Avoidance} = \\text{remove exposure}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Prevention} = \\text{reduce frequency}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Reduction} = \\text{reduce severity}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Credit} = \\text{counterparty fails to pay}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Funding liquidity} = \\text{can't pay now}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Asset liquidity} = \\text{can't sell easily}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Operational} = \\text{people/process/system/control failure}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Business} = \\text{commercial profitability uncertainty}\n}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t1p-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 - The strategy I want you to use in the actual MCQ</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>When two options both seem plausible, ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What exactly is the examiner classifying?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"A poor model causes bad loans.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Could be:</p>\n<p><strong>Credit risk</strong> - because borrowers default.</p>\n<p>or</p>\n<p><strong>Model risk</strong> - because the model is defective.</p>\n<p>If the question asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What caused the losses?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Model/operational risk</span></div>\n<p>If it asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What risk arises because customers may not repay?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Credit risk</span></div>\n<p>That distinction between <strong>root cause, event and consequence</strong> is probably the single strongest technique you can use for difficult Topic 1 MCQs.</p>\n<p>For revision, I would target roughly <strong>20% definition questions, 50% application questions and 30% scenario/analysis questions</strong>. If you can consistently get the application and analysis questions right, ordinary definition MCQs should be almost free marks.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"t1-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, auditors, boards, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the topic&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"1\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Topic 1 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"1\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which wording matches the broader (ISO 31000) definition of risk used in the slides?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The effect of uncertainty on objectives</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The variability in expected return on an investment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The likelihood of experiencing harm or losing something of value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A situation in which a loss is possible regardless of whether it occurs</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>ISO 31000 defines risk as the <strong>effect of uncertainty on objectives</strong>, where the effect may be positive, negative or a deviation. C is the traditional definition, B the finance definition, and D defines a <strong>loss exposure</strong>, not risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>\"The probable size of the losses that may occur\" defines:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Loss frequency</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Loss severity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Loss exposure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Expected loss</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Severity is the <strong>size</strong> of losses. Frequency is the probable <strong>number</strong> of losses in a period. Exposure is the situation in which loss is possible, and expected loss combines frequency and severity.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which set lists only synonyms the slides use for <strong>diversifiable</strong> risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pure, particular, speculative</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Systematic, fundamental, market risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Non-systematic, particular, firm-specific</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Non-systematic, fundamental, firm-specific</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Diversifiable risk is also called <strong>non-systematic, particular or firm-specific</strong> risk. \"Systematic\" and \"fundamental\" describe non-diversifiable risk, so B and D mix the two sides; A mixes in outcome-structure terms.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why can an unexpectedly <strong>good</strong> outcome still be a concern for risk management under the broader definition?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because risk management only measures negative deviations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because positive outcomes are pure risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because good outcomes are always followed by losses</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because any deviation from objectives, including a positive one, reflects uncertainty that can create new problems such as capacity shortages</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Risk is the effect of uncertainty on objectives, positive or negative. A demand surge can cause stock-outs, quality problems and supplier strain. A contradicts the broader definition; C is not a rule; B misuses \"pure risk\", which has no gain possibility.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>What distinguishes risk from uncertainty in the slides?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Risk involves losses; uncertainty involves gains</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risk applies to firms; uncertainty applies to individuals</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Under risk, outcomes are known and odds may be known; under uncertainty, even the outcomes may not be known</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Risk is always insurable; uncertainty is never insurable</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The distinction is about <strong>information</strong>: under risk the outcome set is known and probabilities may be estimable; under uncertainty the outcomes themselves may be unknown. The other options invent distinctions not in the slides.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does the market compensate investors for systematic risk but not for unsystematic risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Unsystematic risk can be removed cheaply through diversification, so bearing it is a choice the market does not reward</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Systematic risk is always larger</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Unsystematic risk cannot be measured</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Regulators prohibit pricing unsystematic risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Investors could diversify firm-specific risk away themselves, so the market prices only the risk they cannot avoid: systematic risk, measured by beta. B is not necessarily true; C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A warehouse installs automatic sprinklers and fire doors. Which technique, and which dimension does it target?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Loss prevention; frequency</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Loss reduction; frequency</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Avoidance; frequency</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Loss reduction; severity</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Sprinklers and fire doors limit damage <strong>once a fire has started</strong>, so they reduce <strong>severity</strong>. They do not stop fires starting (that would be prevention), and the exposure is retained, so it is not avoidance.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A bank lends RM500,000 to a developer. Which risk does the bank face <strong>because of the loan itself</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Funding liquidity risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Credit risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Market directional risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Resource risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Ask \"who owes whom money?\" The developer owes the bank, so the bank faces <strong>credit risk</strong>: the chance of non-payment or late payment. Funding liquidity risk is about the bank's own ability to pay its obligations.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>An investor holds shares in 80 companies across unrelated industries. Bank Negara raises interest rates sharply and almost all holdings fall. Which risk materialised?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Diversifiable risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Particular risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Pure risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Non-diversifiable (systematic) risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>An economy-wide rate shock affects all firms, so diversification cannot remove it: <strong>systematic</strong> risk. Diversifiable and particular risk are firm-specific; pure risk is about outcome structure, and share investment is speculative.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A flawed credit-scoring model approves many borrowers who later default. What is the <strong>root</strong> risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Credit risk, because borrowers defaulted</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Operational (model) risk, with credit losses as the consequence</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Market risk, because loan values fell</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reputational risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The slides place <strong>model risk</strong> under operational risk. The defaults look like credit losses, but the cause is a flawed internal model. Root cause and consequence are different; A names only the consequence.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A company owns RM100m of property, holds RM50,000 cash and must pay RM2m tomorrow. The property would take months to sell. Which pair of risks best describes the position?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Credit risk and market risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Business risk and resource risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Funding liquidity risk and asset liquidity risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Systematic risk and pure risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>It may not meet obligations when due (<strong>funding</strong> liquidity) and cannot sell the asset quickly without loss (<strong>asset</strong> liquidity). Wealth ≠ liquidity. Nobody owes it money (not credit), and the issue is not demand (business).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Risk A is a 1% chance of a RM100m loss; Risk B is a certain RM1m loss. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A may be more dangerous for a small firm because its extreme outcome could threaten survival, despite equal expected loss</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> B is worse because it is certain</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They are equivalent because expected losses are equal</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither matters because expected loss is only RM1m</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Both have expected loss of RM1m, but A's tail outcome could bankrupt a small company. Serious analysis looks at the <strong>distribution and extreme outcomes</strong>, not just averages. C ignores severity; B and D misread the comparison.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A firm considers avoiding all overseas expansion to eliminate political and FX risk. What is the strongest evaluation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Avoidance reduces the chance of those losses to zero but also forgoes the upside; management should compare expected value, risk capacity and the cost of prevention and reduction alternatives</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct: avoidance is always the safest and therefore best choice</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Avoidance is impossible for political risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Avoidance is equivalent to loss prevention</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Avoidance removes the exposure and its upside together. A good decision weighs reward against risk and controls. B treats safety as the only objective; C is false; D confuses the techniques.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>After a boycott, KFC Malaysia temporarily closes outlets. Which answer earns the most marks?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> \"Operational risk, because outlets closed.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> \"Primarily business risk (falling demand from changing customer sentiment), with a reputational component; closures are an operational consequence rather than the source.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> \"Reputational risk.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> \"Credit risk, because revenue fell.\"</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The strong answer separates <strong>cause → risk → consequence</strong>: the demand driver is business risk, brand perception adds reputational risk, and closures are the consequence. A and C pick one word; D misuses credit risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A single-supplier EV-battery maker must choose one control first. Which choice is best justified?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Avoidance: stop using the mineral entirely</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Accept the risk because suppliers rarely fail</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Prevention: qualify additional suppliers so one failure is less likely to stop production, backed by safety stock as reduction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Rely only on insurance after a disruption</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Supplier diversification lowers the <strong>frequency</strong> of production stoppages, and safety stock reduces <strong>severity</strong> if disruption occurs. Avoidance is unrealistic for a core input; B ignores a severe dependency; D addresses only part of the post-loss consequence.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which sequence best designs a risk process for a firm entering a new market, consistent with Topic 1?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Choose treatments → identify exposures → set objectives → monitor</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Classify risks → choose avoidance for all → report</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Buy insurance → assess frequency → set objectives → classify risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Set objectives → identify exposures → classify risks → assess frequency/severity → prioritise → choose treatments → monitor</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Objectives must come first (risk is relative to them), then identification, classification, sizing, prioritisation, treatment and monitoring. The other orders put treatment before understanding, or avoid everything.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You must classify a single factory fire for a board report. Which framing best reflects the chapter's \"classifications overlap\" insight?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Choose exactly one category so the report is unambiguous</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Report it only as speculative risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Present it by dimension: pure (outcome), diversifiable (portfolio view), operational (where it hits), plus liquidity and reputational consequences</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Report it as systematic risk because it affects the economy</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Classifications answer different questions, so one event can carry several labels. Naming the dimension is analysis-level work. A loses information; B and D are wrong classifications.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which identification plan would most completely find exposures in a manufacturing firm?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Combine checklists, physical inspection, flowcharts, financial statements and historical loss data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Review historical losses only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Use the balance sheet only, because all exposures appear there</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ask the insurer to list risks</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Each method looks from a different direction: inspections find blocked exits, flowcharts single suppliers, statements debt exposure, history control weaknesses. Any single source misses something.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Beta is defined as Cov(Ri, Rm) / Var(Rm). Why can a highly volatile stock still have a low beta?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Beta measures co-movement with the market; a stock can swing a lot for firm-specific reasons while barely moving with the market</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Volatility and beta are the same thing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Low beta means low total risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Beta only applies to bonds</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Beta captures systematic sensitivity. Large idiosyncratic swings raise total volatility without raising covariance with the market.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>An airline hedges 70% of next year's jet-fuel needs. In Topic 1 terms, which risk is it mainly managing?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pure risk via loss prevention</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reputational risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Credit risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Market (price) risk, reducing the severity of an adverse price move</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Fuel-price movements are market risk; hedging does not stop prices rising but limits the financial impact, a severity-reducing response. The slides note hedging one speculative exposure does not make it a pure risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Auditor</span></div><p>An internal auditor finds that one clerk can create vendors and approve payments to them. Which risk and control idea is most relevant?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Market risk; diversification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Systematic risk; beta</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Credit risk; collateral</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Operational (fraud) risk; segregate duties as loss prevention</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Combining incompatible duties creates fraud opportunity, an operational risk on the slides. Segregation of duties makes fraud less likely, which is prevention.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A ride-hailing app's servers fail on New Year's Eve. Drivers cannot earn, riders switch to rivals, and the hashtag trends. Which is the best primary classification?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reputational risk, because it trended</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Operational (technology failure) risk as the root, with business and reputational consequences</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Market risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Systematic risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The source is a technology failure in operations; lost trips and the trending hashtag are consequences. Analysis-level answers name the root, then the cascade.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A fund holds 50 Malaysian palm-oil producers and claims to be \"well diversified\". The flaw?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 50 is too few assets for any diversification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The holdings share the same drivers (commodity prices, weather, regulation), so firm-specific risk does not cancel well</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Diversification only works with bonds</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Palm oil is a pure risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Diversification needs assets that do not all react identically. Quantity alone is not enough when risk drivers are common.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Why does the slides' \"systematic risk\" page describing interlinked institutions and cascading failure cause confusion?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because systematic and systemic risk are identical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because systemic risk is diversifiable</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because it describes systemic (contagion) risk while labelled systematic (market-wide, non-diversifiable) risk; related but distinct concepts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because cascading failure is a pure risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Systematic risk is broad market exposure; systemic risk is failure propagating through a connected system. Follow the lecturer's wording in exams but keep the concepts apart.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Auditor</span></div><p>A firm reports \"zero fire losses in ten years\" and argues fire is no longer a risk. The auditor's best response?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Agree: historical data proves the risk is gone</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Disagree: the loss exposure still exists; low historical frequency does not remove a severe exposure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Agree, but only for pure risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Disagree, because fire is speculative</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A loss exposure exists regardless of whether a loss has occurred. Rare, severe events are exactly what historical averages understate.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Board</span></div><p>A board debates a project with expected return 15% and moderate risk versus 4% and low risk. Which board question is most consistent with Topic 1?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> \"Which project has lower risk?\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> \"Which project avoids all uncertainty?\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> \"How does each affect our objectives, and can we bear the downside of the 15% project?\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> \"Which project is easiest to insure?\"</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Risk only makes sense relative to objectives; the decision weighs upside against downside and capacity. A and B treat risk as purely bad; D narrows the question to one tool.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>A researcher proposes ranking risks by expected loss (frequency x severity) only. The strongest critique?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Expected loss is too hard to compute</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Severity cannot be estimated</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Frequency is irrelevant to risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It ignores the distribution: low-frequency, high-severity events with equal expected loss may threaten survival, so tail measures and capacity matter</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Equal averages can hide catastrophic tails (Risk A vs Risk B in the notes). Survival depends on extreme outcomes relative to capacity, not just the mean.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Under the broader definition, should an ERM framework track upside deviations (opportunities) as rigorously as downside ones?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> No: risk management is only about losses</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only for pure risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Yes, to the extent they affect objectives: unmanaged upside can create capacity, quality and supply problems, and missed opportunities are value lost</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only if regulators require it</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Risk is the effect of uncertainty on objectives, positive or negative. Ignoring upside leaves the organisation unprepared and blind to opportunity.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Board</span></div><p>Which dashboard design best follows the Topic 1 risk chain for a board?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> For each key objective: exposures, events, frequency/severity estimates, consequences, chosen control (avoid/prevent/reduce) and owner</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A list of risk names coloured red, amber or green</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only last year's actual losses</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only the insurance schedule</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Objective → exposure → event → frequency/severity → consequence → response is the chain the chapter builds. B, C and D capture fragments.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Design principle: a firm wants one taxonomy that avoids double counting yet captures cascades. Which approach fits Topic 1 best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Classify each event by its root source, record its outcome structure and diversifiability as attributes, and link downstream consequences as separate, connected entries</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Assign every event to exactly one category and ignore consequences</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Use only pure vs speculative</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Use only enterprise categories</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Separating root source from consequences, while keeping the other dimensions as attributes, preserves the \"classifications answer different questions\" insight and supports cascade analysis without double counting.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
